# {{ exercise_title }}

A CPDSE student lab-exercise app for {{ course_code }}. Part of the lab-exercise-app family:
identical look and behaviour across exercises, durable results across years.

## What it does

Student groups record their measurements for one lab exercise, compare with the rest of the
class and with previous years, and export a report they keep.

- Students enter a **course password** (no personal account), register with their **KUID**,
  name and hold, then join or create a group.
- **Data capture** records measurements; **Data analysis** shows their data, an anonymised
  comparison at a scope they choose (own, group, neighbouring group, hold, year, all years),
  the exercise's plots, and the teacher's questions with free-text answer fields.
- Every plot has a **"Show the code"** panel: plain pandas + plotly a student can paste into a
  notebook and run against their exported CSV. These are pre-coding courses, so seeing the
  code is part of the point.
- **Export** produces CSV, Excel, a PDF report and an HTML report. The report combines the
  group's data, their answers and the plots — apps get retired and passwords get forgotten,
  so the export is what students keep.
- Results are **append-only**: a mistake is fixed with a correction that supersedes the old
  reading. "Resetting for a new class" closes the year and opens a new one — nothing is
  deleted, and previous years stay visible and exportable.
- An **admin area** manages course metadata (banner, FAQ, links, permitted comparison scope),
  years, groups and exports.

## How to use it

For students:

- **First visit, two steps.** Enter the course password, then register with your KUID (three
  letters and three digits), your name and your hold, and join or create your group. There is
  no menu until you have registered — until then there is only one thing to do, and nothing
  you entered could be attributed to a group anyway.
- **After that, the exercise.** You land on **Data capture** to record measurements, with
  **Data analysis** to compare, answer the questions and download your report, and **FAQ**
  for the instructions. Those three are the whole menu.
- A later visit skips both steps: the session in your browser URL restores the password *and*
  your identity, so you come back weeks later straight into the exercise while writing up.
- **My group** (to check or change your group) and **Admin** are tucked under **More** in the
  sidebar, because neither is part of doing the exercise.

For the instructor / admin:

- Open **More → Admin** in the sidebar and sign in with the admin password (`ADMIN_PASSWORD`
  at deploy time). Each
  section of the Admin page explains itself in the app; in short:
  - **Course name and instructor** — shown on the FAQ page and on every exported report, so a
    student who finds a PDF months later knows where it came from.
  - **Message to the class** — a banner on *every* page for *every* student, for things that
    change mid-session ("use fridge C, A is broken"). Save an empty box to remove it.
  - **FAQ** — Markdown, for the questions that come back every year. Add to it during the
    session; when two groups ask the same thing, that's the signal.
  - **Comparison scope** — the widest comparison students may pick on the analysis page.
    Comparison views are anonymised regardless. Leave at *all* unless you want groups working
    independently first.
  - **Groups on/off** — turn off for individual exercises; don't change it mid-course.
  - **Documents** — upload the **øvelsesvejledning** and anything else students should be able
    to download (see below).
- Open a year for the new class, close it when done, and open the next.
- Export per year or across all years: **CSV** and **Excel** (measurements), **Answers CSV**,
  a **full workbook** (results · answers · roster · years · log), and a **SQLite backup**.
  Take a backup before closing a year or retiring the app. The backup, the roster and the
  answers contain personal data — store them accordingly.

### Documents students can download

Upload the **øvelsesvejledning** (lab manual), a data sheet or a worked example under
**Admin → Documents**. They appear on the **FAQ page** and in a *Course documents* panel on
the **Data capture** page, so a student who forgot the manual can get it while standing at
the bench. Give each one a title students will recognise and, optionally, a one-line
description ("Read section 3 before you start").

PDF works best; the limit is 20 MB per file — for anything larger, host it elsewhere and put
a link in the FAQ. Files live on the course volume, so they survive redeploys and are still
there next year. They are **not** inside the SQLite backup, so keep your own copies.

### Changing the exercise itself (the seam)

The two pages a teacher defines per course are `exercise/capture.py` (what students enter)
and `exercise/analysis.py` (what they see afterwards). Both ship as **worked examples with
inline instructions** explaining their purpose, what to put in them, and the rules that matter
— read the top of each file before editing. The four seam files:

```
exercise/schema.py     the measurement fields (storage, CSV and exports follow)
exercise/capture.py    the DATA ENTRY page — draw inputs, return a dict
exercise/analysis.py   the ANALYSIS page — return plots built with core.plots
exercise/content.md    the instructions and the analysis questions
```

Run it locally (a writable volume MUST be mounted at the data path):

```
podman build -t {{ project_slug }} .
podman run -p 8000:8000 -v ./lab-data:/asv-mnt/lab-data \
  -e COURSE_PASSWORD=lab2026 -e ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me {{ project_slug }}
```

Then open http://localhost:8000/

## Tech stack

- Python 3.11, Streamlit, pandas, plotly, lmfit, SQLAlchemy 2, Pydantic v2, SQLite (WAL).
- Reports: reportlab (PDF) and plotly (HTML); Excel via openpyxl.
- Single process, single container, port 8000. No Node, no separate API.
- Architecture: `core/` is framework-free Python and imports Streamlit nowhere (a test
  enforces it), `ui/` is the thin Streamlit UI, `exercise/` is the per-app seam, `app.py`
  is the entry point. The UI package is deliberately **not** called `pages/` — that name puts
  Streamlit into magic multipage mode and publishes every internal module as its own URL.
- Visual identity: the fixed CPDSE palette and Verdana typography, defined once in
  `core/theme.py` and applied to both the UI and every plot.

Branding: the CPDSE logo lives in `assets/` and appears deliberately quietly — small in the
page header, at full size only on the sign-in page, and in the footer of exported HTML reports.
To change the artwork, replace the file in `assets/` (see `assets/README.md`); no code changes
are needed, and a missing file degrades to no image rather than an error. Body text is Charcoal
on Soft White / Ivory Gold Tint (both meet WCAG AA); the header uses Soft White on Forest Green
as the identity specifies.

## Dependencies

- A container runtime (Podman or Docker) to build and run the image.
- A persistent, writable AdaLab Shared Volume mounted at the data path — see Access.
- No external services, no database server, no student accounts. Versions are pinned in
