## Access

- Students: no AdaLab account. The **course password** (`COURSE_PASSWORD`, rotated each
  semester at deploy time) is the access control, so `.adalab/app.json` sets
  `access_level: "public"`. Identity is self-registration by KUID — there are no per-student
  passwords.
- Admin: `ADMIN_PASSWORD`, compared constant-time. If it is unset the admin area is disabled
  (fail closed). Rotate by redeploying with a new value.
- Students stay signed in across a page refresh (and when they come back weeks later) via an
  opaque token in the URL — **no student passwords**. Only a hash of it is stored, it carries
  no personal data, it expires after `SESSION_TTL_DAYS`, and "Sign out" revokes it
  immediately. Note the URL is therefore a bearer credential: sharing the link shares the
  session, exactly as sharing a KUID already allows impersonation under the honour-system model.
- Environment variables (complete set): `DATA_DIR` (default `/asv-mnt/lab-data`),
  `COURSE_ID`, `COURSE_PASSWORD`, `ADMIN_PASSWORD`, `DEMO_MODE` (default `false`),
  `APP_PORT` (default 8000), `BASE_URL_PATH` (default the app slug — see URL prefix below),
  `SESSION_TTL_DAYS` (default 30), `LOG_PII` (default `false` — see Logging).

### Logging

Every meaningful action is recorded: student registrations (with timestamps), returning
students, group creation, **every submission**, **every correction** (with the old values, the
new values and which fields changed), answers saved, exports actually taken, all admin actions,
and all errors with their tracebacks.

It goes to three places, because each answers a different question:

- **The `event` table** — filterable by level / action / KUID in **Admin → Log**, downloadable
  as CSV. This is the teacher-facing record of what happened in a lab session.
- **stdout** — lands in the container / AdaLab log viewer, for whoever is on call.
- **`$DATA_DIR/<app>/events.jsonl`** — on the shared volume, so the log survives redeploys
  (stdout does not). Rotated at 5 MB to `events.jsonl.1`.

Personal data handling: the database and the volume log keep the KUID (same data processing
agreement as the results themselves); **stdout gets a pseudonymous `member:<id>` instead**,
because platform logs are aggregated more widely. Set `LOG_PII=true` to include KUIDs in
stdout too. Logging is best-effort by design — a failing log sink can never block a student's
submission.

### URL prefix

AdaLab serves the app at `/apps/<app_url>/` and, with the default **`stripped_prefix: true`**,
removes that prefix before forwarding. The app therefore serves at the **container root** and
is not told any base path.

That works because Streamlit emits *relative* asset URLs (`./static/…`, `./_stcore/stream`):
the browser resolves them against the prefixed page, and the proxy strips the prefix again on
the way in. Verified end to end, including the WebSocket. It also keeps the extension's **Test**
step working, which probes `/` on the container and expects a 2xx.

The two configurations must never be mixed. If a platform ever forwards the prefix intact,
set `BASE_URL_PATH` to that prefix **and** flip `stripped_prefix` to `false` — a test checks
the two stay consistent.

### Local disk instead of a volume

`STORAGE_MODE=local` writes results to `DATA_DIR` as an ordinary directory, creating it if
needed — no volume, no mount, no one-time `chmod`:

```bash
podman run -p 8000:8000 -e STORAGE_MODE=local \
  -e COURSE_PASSWORD=lab2026 -e ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me {{ project_slug }}
```

In AdaLab: set `STORAGE_MODE=local` in the deploy wizard's environment variables and leave
**Volume mounts** empty.

Right for a laptop, a lab, a demo, or a first trial. **Container-local data is erased whenever
the container is replaced** — every redeploy, every restart, every resource change — so the app
warns on every screen, at startup, and on the Export tab, and records `storage_mode` in the
`app_started` event. If you run a real session this way, export from **More → Admin**
afterwards: that download is the only copy.

Without this variable, a missing volume does not abort startup — it **blocks the app**. It
starts and serves (the extension's Test step must be able to probe it), shows the reason on
screen, and never opens the course gate, so not one result can be written to storage that is
about to vanish. That is the guarantee: nothing collectable, rather than nothing running.

### Persistent storage: the Shared Volume (do this before the first class)

Container filesystems are wiped on every redeploy — an **AdaLab Shared Volume (ASV)** is the
only correct place for student data. This app **refuses to start without one**, on purpose:
silently writing to container-local storage would look fine all semester and then lose a year
of results at the next redeploy.

Everything lives under `DATA_DIR` (default `/asv-mnt/lab-data`) in a per-app subdirectory:
`results.sqlite` (system of record), `results.csv` (long-format mirror), `events.jsonl` (the
log), `documents/` (uploads) and `exports/`.

The volume is **not** created by the VS Code extension or by deploying — it is a separate
resource you create once on the Volumes page. Four steps, in this order:

#### 1. Create the volume (Volumes page)

A two-step wizard:

- **Information** — *Name* (descriptive, e.g. `CPDSE Lab Data`), *Description* (which apps use
  it — admin view only), *Size in GB*. **Size is fixed at creation**; only someone with Edit
  rights can resize it later, so allow for years of results plus uploaded documents. A few GB
  is generous for this app: measurements are tiny, uploaded PDFs dominate.
- **Access control** — three independent levels, hierarchical (**Edit ⊃ Mount ⊃ View**), each
  settable to Public / Logged in / Userlist / Groups. You need **Mount** to attach it to the
  app, and **Edit** to resize or change the ACL later.

> **Depending on your institution's setup, only an admin may be able to create volumes.**
> Check this well before the first lab session rather than on the morning.

#### 2. Fix the filesystem permissions — the step everyone misses

A brand-new volume has ACL access but **no filesystem permissions**. The ACL shows green, the
mount succeeds, the wizard is happy — and every write fails with `PermissionError`.

First attach the volume to your **lab** so you can reach it: **burger menu → Volumes**, find
the volume, click **Mount**. In the dialog keep the pre-filled **Mount path** (the volume name
with spaces as underscores), leave **Mount from root** unchecked (so it lands at `~/asv-mnt/…`)
and leave **Read only** unchecked (or the `chmod` below will fail).

This is a different action from mounting the volume into an *app* in the App Deployment
wizard, and the two mount paths are independent — in your lab it is
`~/asv-mnt/<Volume_Name>`, while the app mounts the same volume at `lab-data`. Doing one does
not do the other. A new attachment can take ~2 minutes to appear in a running lab.

Then, in a lab terminal, run it once per volume:

```bash
cd ~/asv-mnt
sudo chown root:$NB_GROUP <Volume_Name>
sudo chmod 775 <Volume_Name>
```

`$NB_GROUP` resolves to `adalab-users` on most installations.

`sudo: unable to send audit message` is harmless container noise — the command still runs.
Check the result rather than the warnings: `ls -ld ~/asv-mnt/<Volume_Name>` should show group
`adalab-users` with group write (`drwxrwsr-x root adalab-users`).

**`<Volume_Name>` is the volume's name with spaces replaced by underscores** — a volume called
`CPDSE Lab Data` appears as `~/asv-mnt/CPDSE_Lab_Data`. `ls ~/asv-mnt` to see the real name.
(If your lab is set to "Mount from Root" the path is `/asv-mnt/...` instead of `~/asv-mnt/...`.)
A newly attached volume can take ~2 minutes to appear in a running lab; wait or restart it.

The two layers are independent and you need both: correct ACL without the chmod means writes
fail; correct chmod without the ACL means you cannot mount at all. *(Expected to become
implicit in AdaLab v1.6, ~mid-2026. Until your platform is upgraded, this is mandatory.)*

#### 3. Mount it into the app (App Deployment wizard)

Add one row under **Volume mounts** on the **primary container**:

| Control | Value for this app |
| --- | --- |
| **Volume** | the ASV you created (the dropdown shows name + size) |
| **Mount path** | `lab-data` — the part **after** `/asv-mnt/`. No leading slash, no trailing slash (a trailing slash is rejected outright). |
| **Read only** | **off** — the app must write |
| **Fast mount** | **on** — see below |

The mount path must match `DATA_DIR`. Ship both as given and they line up at `/asv-mnt/lab-data`;
if you change one, change the other (a test checks they agree). Note this is independent of the
volume's *name* — the name only decides what the directory is called inside a lab.

**Fast Mount is required here, not an optimisation.** ASVs are network filesystems by default;
SQLite over a network mount is slow and can corrupt under load. Fast Mount attaches the volume
directly, removing the network hop. Constraints: **one Fast Mount per app** (this app is
single-container, so it takes it), and it is a property of *this attachment* — the same volume
can be Fast-Mounted here and network-mounted elsewhere.

After deployment you can change a mount in place from the app's kebab menu → **Edit Mount**.

#### 4. Verify

Start the app. If the volume is wrong you get a startup failure naming the path — that is the
guard working, not a bug. When it starts, submit one result, redeploy, and check it is still
there. `Admin → Export → Download database` gives you a backup to keep.

#### Sharing one volume across course apps

One ASV can be mounted into **many apps and labs at once**, which is how a KUID's history stays
reachable across the course's apps: mount the same volume into each, and each app writes to its
own subdirectory (named after its slug). Use the **same mount path** in every app —
different paths for the same data is merely confusing. A volume cannot be mounted twice on the
*same* container.

#### What not to put here

- **Secrets.** `COURSE_PASSWORD` / `ADMIN_PASSWORD` are environment variables set in the deploy
  wizard. Anyone with Mount rights on the volume can read its files.
- **Unbounded growth.** The size is fixed at creation. This app's event log rotates, but
  uploaded documents and years of results do accumulate — check usage between semesters.

Two files appear in every mounted volume and are not yours: `lost+found/` and `.AVI_SUCCESS`.
The app filters them everywhere it lists volume contents.

#### Single replica

SQLite in WAL mode is a single-instance embedded database. **Do not scale this app
horizontally** — two replicas over one volume will corrupt it.

### Deployment order

Deploy in the order **Test → Build → Deploy**. Skipping Build breaks a first-time Deploy. Set
`COURSE_PASSWORD`, `ADMIN_PASSWORD` and the volume mount in the wizard at deploy time — never
commit them to `.adalab/`.

### Before you deploy: check the manifests

```bash
DATA_DIR=$(mktemp -d) python -m pytest tests/test_adalab_config.py -q
```

`.adalab/` is deployment state, so the test suite validates it: container filenames must match
their `uid` field, exactly one primary container, `app_url` within the platform's regex and
length limit, no reserved or secret environment variables, resources inside the caps, and
`stripped_prefix` consistent with how Streamlit is served. Each of these otherwise fails at
build or deploy time. `app_id`, `metadata_id` and `current_image_version` ship as `null` and
are filled in by the deploy flow — **commit them afterwards**, or the next deploy re-derives
stale state.

Note on the URL prefix: the platform rule is that an app is either prefix-aware with
`stripped_prefix: false`, or serves at the root with `stripped_prefix: true` — never mixed.
This app serves at the root, because Streamlit's asset URLs are relative and because the
extension's Test step probes `/`.

```yaml
asset: app
owner: {{ contact_email }}
version: 0.1.0
use: https://<your-adalab-host>/apps/{{ project_slug }}/
source: <repo URL>
data: $DATA_DIR/{{ project_slug }}/results.sqlite (mirror: results.csv, exports in-app)
```
